\begin{abstract}
The Scalar–Angular–Torsion (SAT) theory, also known as SAT/Blockwave, presents a unified geometric framework that rigorously integrates the Standard Model, General Relativity, and Quantum Mechanics through a minimal geometric ontology. Reality is modeled as a 4D network of particle worldlines, termed \textbf{filaments}, propagating through an advancing \textbf{time surface}. Fundamental constants, mass, and force laws emerge solely from the topology and geometry of the filament-timesheet intersection. Adhering to the \textbf{Zero-Parameter Economy} (Normalization Principle), the theory axiomatically introduces \textbf{zero numerical parameters}; all dimensionless constants are structurally derived from internal geometry. An iterative calibration process successfully converged on a dimensionless coupling ratio $\mathbf{R \approx 0.007304}$, matching the fine-structure constant ($\alpha$) with high precision. We present the \textbf{Total Covariant Action} ($S_{\text{SAT}}$), the rigorously derived Euler–Lagrange system, and the emergent \textbf{Topological Mass Suppression Law} ($\mathbf{m \propto 1/Q}$). Stability constraints, termed the \textbf{UV Finiteness Lock}, mandate that only particle configurations with a Topological Charge $\mathbf{Q \leq 3}$ can be dynamically stable ground states in 4D spacetime. Based on this constraint, we predict the existence of two stable, novel ground-state particles: $\mathbf{Q=2}$ ($\approx \mathbf{0.5073\times 10^{-27}\text{ kg}}$) and $\mathbf{Q=3}$ ($\approx \mathbf{0.3403\times 10^{-27}\text{ kg}}$), which serve as primary candidates for dark matter.
\end{abstract}

